WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:17.580
Russia's invasion of Ukraine is not only&nbsp;
radically changing Europe's geopolitics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:21.660
security and worldview, it is also bringing&nbsp;
huge changes for Russia... And as time goes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:25.440
on this will become more and more noticeable.&nbsp;
In a recent video here on VisualPolitik we told&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:29.340
you how the sanctions on Russia are achieving&nbsp;
many of the goals they set out to achieve.
 &nbsp;

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:35.160
In fact, Russia is, in quantitative terms,&nbsp;
the most sanctioned country in the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:40.740
Specifically, 2023 started with 13,600 sanctions&nbsp;
and restrictions of all kinds on the Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:45.000
State, the country's companies as well as many&nbsp;
oligarchs close to the Kremlin. Of these, almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:50.040
11,000 were imposed after the invasion&nbsp;
of Ukraine on 24 February 2022.
 &nbsp;

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:53.640
For the sake of comparison, Iran, the second most&nbsp;
sanctioned country in the world, has just over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:59.460
4,000 sanctions and restrictions, while Syria and&nbsp;
North Korea have 2,600 and 2,100 respectively.
 &nbsp;

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:06.240
And that is not all. Western economies&nbsp;
and in general most of Ukraine's allies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:09.660
have made gigantic efforts to progressively&nbsp;
get rid of everything coming from Russia:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:11.940
its gas, its oil, the money of&nbsp;
the oligarchs... Everything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:15.120
except, one thing: supplies from&nbsp;
the Russian nuclear sector.
 &nbsp;

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:23.460
We are talking specifically about Rosatom,&nbsp;
a huge industrial group owned by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:27.420
Russian government that, for now, has escaped&nbsp;
sanctions. It has been spared for one reason:&nbsp;

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:31.740
Right now, what this company offers is much&nbsp;
more difficult to replace than hydrocarbons.
 &nbsp;

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:34.260
So despite the enormous efforts&nbsp;
Zelenskiy has made morning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:36.360
noon and night to pressure his allies to sanction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:41.520
Rosatom as well, so far he has failed to do so.
 
N1(9 January 2023: Ukraine expects EU to include&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:45.600
Russia's Rosatom in next sanctions – Reuters)
 
And keep in mind, we are talking about none&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:49.500
other than Russia's state-owned atomic&nbsp;
energy company. And that's not all.
 &nbsp;

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:54.900
This nuclear giant is virtually the only Russian&nbsp;
company that we can say is truly on a par with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:59.400
best in the world in its sector, whether in terms&nbsp;
of technology, competitiveness, costs or quality.
 &nbsp;

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:02.580
It is something like the great exception&nbsp;
of the Russian industrial fabric,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:06.360
a productive environment that is, to put it&nbsp;
bluntly, characterized by its inefficiency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:11.160
its excessive corruption and a deep technological&nbsp;
gap with respect to its western counterparts.

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:15.060
But you can see that this is not the case&nbsp;
with this company, quite the contrary.
 &nbsp;

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:18.780
[Of course, the fact that the Western nuclear&nbsp;
industry has been battered by the anti-nuclear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:23.580
policies over recent decades has logically&nbsp;
contributed to Rosatom's gain in market share].
 &nbsp;

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:27.300
Now then... What is Rosatom really, what do&nbsp;
they do specifically, why haven't sanctions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:30.900
been imposed on this company, and could this&nbsp;
change? In other words, are there grounds for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:34.920
sanctioning the Russian nuclear giant? What role&nbsp;
does this company play in the political, economic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:39.120
and military fabric of Vladimir Putin's Russia?
 
Well, in this video we are going to answer all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:46.020
these questions and a few more.
 
Let's get started.
 &nbsp;

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:54.780
T1(PUTIN'S GOLDEN CHILD)
 
As most of you probably already know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:57.540
the fall of the Soviet Union was&nbsp;
followed by a period of several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:01.680
years of deep crisis where a huge economic&nbsp;
and industrial collapse was experienced.
 &nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:05.280
And obviously the nuclear sector was no&nbsp;
exception... Despite the fact that the Soviet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:10.380
Union was a huge power in civil nuclear energy.
 
For example, it was in the USSR that the world's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:15.540
first nuclear power plant to feed electricity&nbsp;
into the grid was born. It did this in 1954.
 &nbsp;

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:19.440
The Obninsk plant, with just 5 MW&nbsp;
of power, made history, but that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:23.280
was only the beginning of a long road ahead.
 
Since then, the Soviets have designed or built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:26.400
more than 60 nuclear reactors distributed&nbsp;
throughout the country and the rest of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:30.120
communist bloc made up of countless satellite&nbsp;
countries. In this way, they gained significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:34.560
know-how and a good reputation, at least&nbsp;
until the Chernobyl nuclear accident in 1986.
 &nbsp;

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:39.660
[Then, not surprisingly, everything changed].
 
But the fact is that when the USSR collapsed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:44.100
the entire industrial fabric of the State also&nbsp;
collapsed. Some of these traditional industries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:47.820
never recovered, others did, but at a much&nbsp;
lower standard than their western competition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:51.000
and others, on the other hand, were&nbsp;
reestablished with care by the government.
 &nbsp;

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:53.820
The latter case was precisely&nbsp;
what happened with Rosatom.
 &nbsp;

00:03:56.460 --> 00:04:04.320
Vladimir Putin himself made it a personal priority&nbsp;
to revive the nuclear industry. To this end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:08.340
in 2006 he enacted a law that sought&nbsp;
to create an entire long-term strategic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:11.820
framework for the nuclear sector, and&nbsp;
shortly thereafter, in 2007, Putin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:16.020
himself founded Rosatom as a State corporation.
 
Behind this decision was the Russian government's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:19.620
concern about the widening gap between&nbsp;
electricity supply and demand. In other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:23.700
words, with Rosatom they intended to solve the&nbsp;
electricity shortage once and for all and thus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:28.200
prevent it from continuing to harm the economy.
 
In addition, during the 1990s, Russia was left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:32.220
out of the international nuclear energy market&nbsp;
because it did not have a reference producer. To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:36.840
give you an idea, between the early 1990s and&nbsp;
the mid-2000s, more than 100 nuclear reactors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:40.680
were built around the globe. Russia was not able&nbsp;
to participate in the construction and design of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:44.880
any of them. In other words, Moscow understood&nbsp;
that it was missing out on big business.
 &nbsp;

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:51.600
So as we say around here,&nbsp;
no sooner said than done.
 &nbsp;

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:56.160
Rosatom was launched with an amazing financial&nbsp;
boost from the Russian State and with Vladimir&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:59.760
Putin one hundred percent involved in&nbsp;
this mission. In fact, it is he himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:03.180
who decides who runs this company and who&nbsp;
also makes the most important appointments.
 &nbsp;

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:11.400
The fact is that with this company, Russian&nbsp;
nuclear energy has once again made headway and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:22.440
today it is already one of the major international&nbsp;
players in the nuclear energy sector.
 &nbsp;

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:26.280
But, to be clearer, it is enough for me to&nbsp;
give you just one fact: of the 53 nuclear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:31.380
reactors that were under construction worldwide in&nbsp;
mid-2022, 20 were being built by Rosatom, and of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:36.060
these, 17 were outside Russia. In other words, we&nbsp;
are talking about a market share of almost 40%.
 &nbsp;

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:40.200
France and South Korea are also building reactors&nbsp;
outside their respective borders, but they are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:43.500
only involved in a couple of projects each.
 
But it doesn't end there. In many cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:47.280
the Russian company is also responsible for the&nbsp;
maintenance and supervision of Russian-technology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:51.180
nuclear reactors around the world. We are talking&nbsp;
about approximately 20 percent of the almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:54.180
450 reactors in operation.
 
In other words, we can safely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:58.140
say that Rosatom, in its little more than 15&nbsp;
years of operations, has managed to dominate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:01.920
the international nuclear market. Or at&nbsp;
least become the most important player.
 &nbsp;

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:06.420
C1("The Russians are hypercompetitive, no one&nbsp;
in the Organization for Economic Cooperation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:11.280
and Development [OECD] can compete with what they&nbsp;
offer". – Marc-Antoine Eyl-Mazzega, director of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:16.200
the energy and climate center at the Institut&nbsp;
Français des Relations Internationales)
 &nbsp;

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:20.400
Surprised? Well, wait, there's still&nbsp;
more, much more. We've only just begun.
 &nbsp;

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:29.400
If you think about it for a moment, the reason&nbsp;
why the West has not sanctioned Rosatom cannot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:33.360
only be its nuclear reactor exports.
 
After all, the United States has no Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:36.780
nuclear reactors, and in Europe, nuclear&nbsp;
energy has been marginalized for years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:39.900
and now that it is making a comeback,&nbsp;
most of the nuclear technology suppliers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:45.240
are Western or South Korean. Not Russian.
 
So why on earth is Rosatom not being sanctioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:49.500
at once? What else is in play for this possibility&nbsp;
to be completely ruled out for the time being?
 &nbsp;

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:53.836
Well, here comes perhaps the most interesting&nbsp;
part of this whole story. Pay close attention.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.836 --> 00:06:53.936
But first, remember that if you like the economy&nbsp;
and the best of VisualPolitik style you can't miss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.936 --> 00:06:54.029
our sister channel VisualEconomik. For example,&nbsp;
have you ever wondered what will happen when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.029 --> 00:06:54.870
oil runs out - which according to predictions&nbsp;
will be in about 50 years? We leave a link in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.870 --> 00:07:02.640
the description for you to check it out. 
 
T2(ATOMS MADE IN RUSSIA)
 &nbsp;

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:09.960
Financial sanctions, restrictions on&nbsp;
technology purchases, sanctions on Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:13.260
gas and oil were imposed relatively quickly.&nbsp;
However, at the time of making this video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:16.500
Rosatom had managed to fend off&nbsp;
any punishment or restrictions.
 &nbsp;

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:20.700
And this obviously must have an explanation.
 
And it does: this company not only designs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:24.900
and builds nuclear reactors,&nbsp;
but also enriches uranium.&nbsp;

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:32.520
And although we can find major Western&nbsp;
suppliers here, Rosatom is the world's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:35.760
largest producer of enriched uranium,&nbsp;
with about 30 percent of production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:40.260
and almost half of the world's capacity.
 
In addition, it controls 17 percent of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:42.960
global nuclear reactor fuel market.&nbsp;
And according to the company itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:47.820
73 nuclear reactors in 13 different countries run&nbsp;
on its fuel. And we are not just talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:52.800
the former Soviet nuclear power plants.
 
In 2021, for example, Rosatom provided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:56.460
nearly a quarter of all nuclear fuel&nbsp;
used by US nuclear power plants.
 &nbsp;

00:07:56.460 --> 00:07:59.940
What’s more, it is estimated that even&nbsp;
in a scenario of supplier switching,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:04.140
the Russians will retain at least 15 percent&nbsp;
of the pie in the United States in the short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:08.760
to medium term. This is because it is a very&nbsp;
slow and costly process to specialize in nuclear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:16.920
fuel production at a reasonable scale and price.
 
Clearly its market share in Europe is not minor.
 &nbsp;

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:24.660
The Russians provide around 20 percent of&nbsp;
all uranium imported into the European Union,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:28.800
behind only Niger and Kazakhstan, as&nbsp;
well as 26 percent of enriched uranium.
 &nbsp;

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:32.640
In fact, from time to time, Russian cargo&nbsp;
planes can still be seen in European skies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:36.660
flying to countries like Hungary or Slovakia&nbsp;
with nuclear fuel, thanks precisely to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.660 --> 00:08:40.080
exceptions that were introduced to the ban on&nbsp;
the entry of Russian aircraft. If they carry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:42.960
nuclear fuel they can fly freely.
 
But why is all this so important,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:46.200
and is it really so essential to keep&nbsp;
Rosatom free from Western sanctions?
 &nbsp;

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:49.260
Well... the truth is yes, and it&nbsp;
may not be exactly what you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:57.000
thinking. Let's take a look at it.
 
T3(STRATEGIC INFLUENCE)
 &nbsp;

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:05.280
Rosatom is a huge company with a large&nbsp;
portfolio of contracts in the nuclear sector.
 &nbsp;

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:10.440
To give you an idea, in 2021 it reported&nbsp;
record profits of $20 billion. So we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:14.700
not exactly talking about a small company.
 
And not only that, in that same year the holding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:20.460
company, Atomenergoprom, in which a large part&nbsp;
of Rosatom's activity is unified, had a portfolio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:25.260
of foreign orders of close to $140 billion&nbsp;
dollars to be executed over the next decade.
 &nbsp;

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:29.460
And yes, some of the nuclear reactor construction&nbsp;
contracts that swell that huge number of projects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.460 --> 00:09:33.701
are in NATO countries, some of them are even&nbsp;
supposed allies of Ukraine. Check it out.
 &nbsp;

00:09:33.701 --> 00:09:37.740
N2(27 August 2022: Hungary moves ahead with&nbsp;
construction of Russian-built nuclear reactors
 &nbsp;

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:42.840
Despite Moscow's war in Ukraine, works at the&nbsp;
Paks plant will kick off in 'coming weeks,'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:47.040
Hungary's foreign minister said – Politico)
 
N3(30 July 2022: Russia's Rosatom signs new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:51.360
construction contract for Turkish nuclear plant
 
The Turkish government aims to start operating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:57.240
the first reactor at the total 4,800-megawatt&nbsp;
(MW) plant before a general election next year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.240 --> 00:10:02.520
President Tayyip Erdogan has previously suggested&nbsp;
that Turkey could work with Russia on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.520 --> 00:10:07.140
construction of two further plants. – Reuters)
 
So, reactor construction and maintenance is by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:15.300
far the most lucrative business for Rosatom.
 
In fact, let us give you another fact. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:18.720
annual imports of uranium and nuclear fuel&nbsp;
from Russia by the United States and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:22.560
European Union are essential but amount&nbsp;
to barely $1 billion dollars a year.
 &nbsp;

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:26.640
Yes, it is a lot of money, but it is nothing&nbsp;
compared to the nearly $200 billion that were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:30.060
imported from Russia in hydrocarbons&nbsp;
just before the war. And neither does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:33.240
it come close to what Rosatom earns from&nbsp;
its reactor and power plant business.
 &nbsp;

00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:36.480
So, with all this, you may be wondering...&nbsp;
so couldn't Rosatom be sanctioned to prevent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:39.900
it from being able to build new reactors&nbsp;
and power plants, while continuing to buy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.900 --> 00:10:43.740
uranium and nuclear fuel from them until&nbsp;
those supplies can be fully replaced?
 &nbsp;

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:48.120
Well, yes, it could, and that would probably&nbsp;
hurt them the most. But there are two drawbacks.
 &nbsp;

00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:55.140
On the one hand, taking this step in a market&nbsp;
as restricted and with few suppliers like this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.140 --> 00:10:58.140
one might make Russia itself willing&nbsp;
to sacrifice those revenues in order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.140 --> 00:11:02.160
to leave the West without its nuclear fuel.
 
And this, in the United States and Europe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:06.480
could cause real supply problems for power&nbsp;
plants at a time of high hydrocarbon prices.
 &nbsp;

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:13.140
And then there is another problem, and that is&nbsp;
that there are not so many suppliers of nuclear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:16.320
technology for modern power plants.
 
In other words, banning imports of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:19.500
these technologies would probably&nbsp;
generate a bottleneck of orders to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:23.340
the civil nuclear industry in countries such&nbsp;
as the United States, France and South Korea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:28.020
that would threaten the world's energy supply.
 
And it would do so precisely in what is expected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.020 --> 00:11:32.520
to be the greatest period of electrification in&nbsp;
the history of mankind due to the fulfillment of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.520 --> 00:11:36.900
climate objectives, something that, by the way, we&nbsp;
have already talked about here on VisualPolitik.
 &nbsp;

00:11:39.960 --> 00:11:44.340
So the reality is that, like it or not, the&nbsp;
United States and the European Union will have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.340 --> 00:11:48.600
to be very cautious about imposing sanctions&nbsp;
against Rosatom, and that is precisely why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:54.060
Rosatom has so far escaped unscathed.
 
Now, is this irreversible? Well, no,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.060 --> 00:11:57.960
of course not. In fact, both the United States&nbsp;
and the European Union already have plans to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.960 --> 00:12:01.140
boost their production of enriched&nbsp;
uranium and fuel for power plants.
 &nbsp;

00:12:01.140 --> 00:12:05.340
N4(US Redoubles Efforts to End&nbsp;
Dependence on Russian Nuclear Fuel
 &nbsp;

00:12:05.340 --> 00:12:10.140
The White House is prioritizing development&nbsp;
of domestic uranium-enrichment capacity and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.140 --> 00:12:14.040
predicted key lawmakers will fall in&nbsp;
line, so that a fuel-manufacturing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.040 --> 00:12:20.160
plan can be in train by 2025 – Bloomberg)
 
N5(Nuclear included in EU's repowering plan
 &nbsp;

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:26.100
The European Commission has formally adopted the&nbsp;
REPowerEU plan which aims to rapidly reduce EU&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.100 --> 00:12:31.380
dependence on Russian fossil fuels. The plan [...]&nbsp;
highlights the importance of coordinated action to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.380 --> 00:12:35.160
reduce dependence on Russian nuclear materials&nbsp;
and fuel cycle services. – World Nuclear News)
 &nbsp;

00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:39.120
But of course, until all that&nbsp;
materializes, Rosatom will remain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.120 --> 00:12:42.960
of crucial importance for its supply.
 
The risk now is that dependence on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.960 --> 00:12:46.860
West will allow this company to become a sort of&nbsp;
by-pass for sanctions and divert technologies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.860 --> 00:12:49.860
supply purchases and profits to&nbsp;
finance the military campaign.
 &nbsp;

00:12:52.680 --> 00:12:57.660
For the time being, Rosatom has already&nbsp;
actively participated in the invasion of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.660 --> 00:13:03.840
Ukraine, as it is the current operator of the&nbsp;
Zaporizhzhia nuclear power plant and also its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:08.040
de facto owner as long as it remains occupied.
 
In addition, the company is acting on the orders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.040 --> 00:13:11.460
of the Russian government in operating this&nbsp;
Ukrainian power plant, which is the largest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.460 --> 00:13:14.820
in Europe with a total of six reactors.
 
Since the beginning of the occupation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:18.540
the power plant, units have been being switched&nbsp;
on and off at the whim of the Kremlin to undermine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.540 --> 00:13:21.660
the morale of the Ukrainians and prevent&nbsp;
them from earning money through the export&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:28.260
of electricity to their European neighbors.
 
In fact, this is one of the main arguments used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.260 --> 00:13:31.920
